## 1. Primitive Schema

All cryptographic behavior is expressed over a shared schema:

- S — state space (messages, ciphertexts, group elements)
- K — key space (symmetric keys, keypairs, parameters)
- P — parameter space (block size, curve, modulus, rounds)
- θ — mode selector

Mode set:

- θ ∈ { aes256, ecc, rsa, hybrid }

---

## 2. Unified Equation

The core transform of the engine is:

\[
C = F_{\theta}(M, K, P)
\]

with inverse:

\[
M = F_{\theta}^{-1}(C, K, P)
\]

Where:

- \(M \in S\) — message/state  
- \(C \in S\) — ciphertext/transformed state  
- \(K \in K\) — key material  
- \(P \in P\) — parameters  
- \(\theta\) — mode selector  
- \(F_{\theta}\) — concrete operator family for mode θ  

This is the **governing equation**.  
AES‑256, ECC, and RSA are **instantiations** of \(F_{\theta}\) under different algebraic regimes.

---

## 3. Mode Instantiation

### 3.1 AES‑256 Mode

\[
C = F_{\text{AES}}(M, K_{256}, P_{\text{block,rounds}})
\]

- State: bitstrings in \(\{0,1\}^{128}\) (blocks)  
- Key: \(K_{256} \in \{0,1\}^{256}\)  
- Params: block size, key size, rounds  

### 3.2 ECC Mode

\[
C = F_{\text{ECC}}(M, k_{\text{scalar}}, P_{\text{curve}})
\]

- State: points on elliptic curve \(E(\mathbb{F}_p)\)  
- Key: scalar \(k_{\text{scalar}} \in \mathbb{Z}_n\)  
- Params: curve definition  

### 3.3 RSA Mode

\[
C = F_{\text{RSA}}(M, (n,e), P_{\text{mod}})
\]

- State: integers in \(\mathbb{Z}_n\)  
- Key: public/private key components \((n,e,d)\)  
